From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nabeel Sowan <nabeel@vibes.se>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 16:07:23 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] tdehw: take the form factor from the SMBIOS chassis type

The root device's form factor was a guess: Laptop if a backlight device
was already listed, Desktop otherwise. On a Dell Latitude 5430, a
notebook (chassis type 10) with an intel_backlight, it came out as
Desktop, because the backlight is not reliably enumerated by the time
the root device is classified. Anything keyed on it then treats the
laptop as a desktop. tdepowersave hides every lid-close setting when
isLaptop() is false.

The firmware already says what the machine is, and the kernel exposes
it world-readable in /sys/class/dmi/id/chassis_type. Map that to
Desktop, Laptop or Server, grouping the SMBIOS types as
systemd-hostnamed does. Tablets, convertibles and detachables count as
laptops, since they carry a battery and usually a lid. Fall back to
the backlight guess only where the firmware reports nothing useful.

Two consumers were checked: the hardware manager's properties dialog,
which already displays every value including Server, and tdepowersave's
isLaptop(). Nothing tests for Desktop, so a server now reported as
Server loses nothing.
---
 tdecore/tdehw/tdehardwaredevices.cpp | 52 +++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 47 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/tdecore/tdehw/tdehardwaredevices.cpp b/tdecore/tdehw/tdehardwaredevices.cpp
index a61cfb77..5d268517 100644
--- a/tdecore/tdehw/tdehardwaredevices.cpp
+++ b/tdecore/tdehw/tdehardwaredevices.cpp
@@ -3371,11 +3371,53 @@ void TDEHardwareDevices::updateExistingDeviceInformation(TDEGenericDevice *devic
 		// Try to obtain as much generic information about this system as possible
 		TDERootSystemDevice* rdevice = dynamic_cast<TDERootSystemDevice*>(device);
 
-		// Guess at my form factor
-		// dmidecode would tell me this, but is somewhat unreliable
-		TDESystemFormFactor::TDESystemFormFactor formfactor = TDESystemFormFactor::Desktop;
-		if (listByDeviceClass(TDEGenericDeviceType::Backlight).count() > 0) {	// Is this really a good way to determine if a machine is a laptop?
-			formfactor = TDESystemFormFactor::Laptop;
+		// Take the form factor from the SMBIOS chassis type, which the kernel
+		// exposes world-readable. The grouping follows systemd-hostnamed's.
+		// Only where the firmware says nothing useful fall back to guessing
+		// from a backlight, which is not reliably enumerated by the time the
+		// root device is.
+		TDESystemFormFactor::TDESystemFormFactor formfactor = TDESystemFormFactor::Unclassified;
+		bool chassisok = false;
+		int chassis = readLineFile("/sys/class/dmi/id/chassis_type").stripWhiteSpace().toInt(&chassisok);
+		if (chassisok) {
+			switch (chassis) {
+				case 0x03:	// Desktop
+				case 0x04:	// Low-profile desktop
+				case 0x05:	// Pizza box
+				case 0x06:	// Mini tower
+				case 0x07:	// Tower
+				case 0x0D:	// All in one
+				case 0x0F:	// Space-saving
+				case 0x10:	// Lunch box
+				case 0x18:	// Sealed-case PC
+				case 0x23:	// Mini PC
+				case 0x24:	// Stick PC
+					formfactor = TDESystemFormFactor::Desktop;
+					break;
+				case 0x08:	// Portable
+				case 0x09:	// Laptop
+				case 0x0A:	// Notebook
+				case 0x0E:	// Sub notebook
+				case 0x1E:	// Tablet
+				case 0x1F:	// Convertible
+				case 0x20:	// Detachable
+					formfactor = TDESystemFormFactor::Laptop;
+					break;
+				case 0x11:	// Main server chassis
+				case 0x17:	// Rack mount chassis
+				case 0x1C:	// Blade
+				case 0x1D:	// Blade enclosure
+					formfactor = TDESystemFormFactor::Server;
+					break;
+				default:	// Other, unknown, or a type without a clear answer
+					break;
+			}
+		}
+		if (formfactor == TDESystemFormFactor::Unclassified) {
+			formfactor = TDESystemFormFactor::Desktop;
+			if (listByDeviceClass(TDEGenericDeviceType::Backlight).count() > 0) {
+				formfactor = TDESystemFormFactor::Laptop;
+			}
 		}
 		rdevice->internalSetFormFactor(formfactor);
 
